      <p>The most common non-invasive imaging tool. An ultrasound uses sound waves to create an image of the liver. A fatty liver appears "bright" or has increased echogenicity compared to normal liver tissue. Ultrasounds are excellent for identifying the grade of fat accumulation (Grades 1, 2, or 3) but cannot accurately measure the degree of scarring (fibrosis).</p>
      
